# The integers, built from the naturals as signed counts, and the unique totally ordered rung of the whole tower. In
# any ordered ring every square is at least zero, because a positive times a positive and a negative times a negative
# are both positive. The integers satisfy this and lie on a line. The moment the tower builds outward a square root of
# minus one appears, its square is below zero, and no order can place it, so every higher rung is unorderable and
# stays so. An order runs one way, and that one-wayness, the count having a before and an after that cannot be
# reversed, is the arrow of time. This is the first object Vibe Theory rests on.

# The arrow of time, proof-checked by `seed test`. The integers carry a one-way (reflexive, transitive,
# antisymmetric) order, and every square is non-negative.

# UNIVERSAL order laws, true for EVERY integer (the parameters are quantified), proved by the linear prover.

# The order is reflexive: every integer is at least itself, n >= n, for every n.
task order-reflexivity
  take n, like integer
  like integer
  hold <every integer is at least itself>
    call is-minimum
      loan n
      loan n
  send back
    loan n

# The order is translation-invariant, the arrow's engine: adding one strictly increases, n < n + 1, for every n. So
# the count always has a next, an after that is never a before, which is the one-wayness of time.
task successor-increases
  take n, like integer
  like integer
  hold <every integer is below its successor>
    call is-below
      loan n
      call add
        loan n
        mark 1
  send back
    loan n

# The order is transitive: if a is at most b and b is at most c, then a is at most c, for every a, b, c.
task order-transitivity
  take a, like integer
  take b, like integer
  take c, like integer
  like integer
  fork test
    hook test
      call is-maximum
        loan a
        loan b
    hook hold
      fork test
        hook test
          call is-maximum
            loan b
            loan c
        hook hold
          hold <the order is transitive>
            call is-maximum
              loan a
              loan c
  send back
    loan a

# Addition preserves the order (translation invariance): if a is at most b then a + c is at most b + c, for every
# a, b, c. This is what makes the order a ring order and the arrow a consistent direction.
task addition-monotone
  take a, like integer
  take b, like integer
  take c, like integer
  like integer
  fork test
    hook test
      call is-maximum
        loan a
        loan b
    hook hold
      hold <addition preserves the order>
        call is-maximum
          call add
            loan a
            loan c
          call add
            loan b
            loan c
  send back
    loan a

# The loss of order, the arrow's far wall: a value whose square is at least zero (the orderability every ordered ring
# demands) cannot also be a square root of minus one. Assume both and a contradiction follows, so no order can place
# the new unit, and every rung past the integers is unorderable. The one-way order stops here, and that is the arrow.
task no-order-past-the-integers
  take square, like integer
  like integer
  fork test
    hook test
      call is-minimum
        loan square
        mark 0
    hook hold
      fork test
        hook test
          call is-equal
            loan square
            mark -1
        hook hold
          hold <orderability fails once a square is minus one>
            call is-below
              mark 0
              mark -1
  send back
    loan square

# Every square is non-negative, for EVERY integer n: n * n >= 0. This is the law no higher rung satisfies, so the
# integers are the unique orderable rung. A universal law, proved by the sum-of-squares certificate (a square is at
# least zero over any ordered ring).
task every-square-is-non-negative
  take n, like integer
  like integer
  hold <every square is at least zero>
    call is-minimum
      call multiply
        loan n
        loan n
      mark 0
  send back
    loan n

# The order is one-way (strict and antisymmetric): 2 is below 5, and conversely 5 is at least 2, so 5 is not below 2.
hold <two is below five>
  call is-below
    mark 2
    mark 5
hold <five is at least two>
  call is-minimum
    mark 5
    mark 2
